<script>
import Code from '#src/lib/Code.abide'
import Doc from '#src/lib/Doc.abide'
import Example from '#src/lib/Example.abide'
import Section from '#src/lib/Section.abide'

import FilterCount from './lib/FilterCount.abide'
import filterCount from './lib/FilterCount.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import SeeTheMessage from './lib/SeeTheMessage.abide'
import seeTheMessage from './lib/SeeTheMessage.abide' with { type: 'text' }
</script>

<Doc
    seeAlso={['/components/toasts-api', '/helpers/chart']}
    tests={[
        {
            path: 'packages/dogfood/examples/crm/src/reports/lib/WonRevenue.test.ts',
            from: [
                'test("a move the keys make is read out, and a pointer\'s is not"',
            ],
        },
        {
            path: 'packages/abide/tests/announce.test.ts',
            from: [
                "test('a message replaces the last, and the same words count as a new message'",
            ],
        },
        {
            path: 'packages/abide/tests/announce.test.ts',
            from: ["test('announce throws inside a request — 50.50'"],
        },
        {
            path: 'packages/dogfood/examples/crm/src/reports/lib/WonRevenue.test.ts',
            from: [
                "test('the same words said twice land twice, each replacing the last'",
            ],
        },
        {
            path: 'packages/abide/tests/announce.test.ts',
            from: [
                "test('a message with no announce.host standing warns once — 50.36'",
            ],
        },
    ]}
>
    <Section level={3} title="Say how many deals a filter left">
        <p>A list narrowing as you type changes nothing a screen reader is reading. Announce the
            count, and it is read out once the typing pauses.</p>
        <Example code={filterCount}><FilterCount /></Example>
    </Section>

    <Section level={3} title="Read every announcement out from one place">
        <p>Call <code>announce.host</code> from your root layout's script and spread its
            <code>polite</code> and <code>assertive</code> parts onto two elements there, each
            holding what <code>said</code> hands back for it, keyed by its <code>count</code>. Hide
            them with a class. Both regions stand empty from the first paint, which is what lets a
            screen reader hear the first message that lands in them.</p>
        <Code language="abide" caption="abide" code={`
            <script>
            import { announce } from '@abide/abide'

            const regions = announce.host()
            </script>

            <html lang="en">
                <body>
                    <slot />
                    <div class="sr-only">
                        <div {...regions.polite}>
                            {#for message of regions.said('polite') by message.count}
                                <span>{message.text}</span>
                            {/for}
                        </div>
                        <div {...regions.assertive}>
                            {#for message of regions.said('assertive') by message.count}
                                <span>{message.text}</span>
                            {/for}
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </body>
            </html>
        `} />
    </Section>

    <Section level={3} title="Interrupt for what cannot wait">
        <p><code>politeness: 'assertive'</code> cuts in on whatever is being read. Keep it for what
            the reader has to know now. The next example runs it.</p>
        <Code language="abide" caption="abide" code={`
            <script>
            import { announce } from '@abide/abide'
            </script>

            <button
                onclick={() =>
                    announce('Connection lost', { politeness: 'assertive' })}
            >Drop the connection</button>
        `} />
    </Section>

    <Section level={3} title="See the message while you build">
        <p>Leave the hiding class off to read what a screen reader is told. Each message lands in
            the region its politeness names and replaces the one before it, so neither grows into a
            log. A page shows its messages in the last host it renders, so the count the filter
            above announces shows here too.</p>
        <Example code={seeTheMessage}><SeeTheMessage /></Example>
    </Section>
</Doc>
